item.content:encoded:
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Built a full-stack web app using React, Node.js and PostgreSQL.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's true, but it tells the reader almost nothing. Thousands of people have built a React app. A recruiter wants to know what problem you solved, which parts you did yourself, and how they can tell it worked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I write project bullets by answering three questions, using evidence that's already in the repo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;**&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The three questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;**&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What problem does the project solve? Answer in one sentence that someone who has never seen the code would understand.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What did you personally do? This matters most when other people touched the project.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What evidence is there? Tests, a live deploy, a README, a measured result: anything a reviewer could check.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A bullet that answers fewer than two of these is really just a list of technologies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


A worked example
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The candidate and project below are made up for this post.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sara built ShiftSwap, a small app that lets staff at a café trade shifts. Her README says:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The café's 12 staff used a WhatsApp group for swaps, and requests got lost&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;App built with Next.js and PostgreSQL, deployed on Vercel&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Manager approves swaps with one click; both people get an email&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;41 tests covering swap rules (no double-booking, no overtime past 40 hrs)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Used by the café for 3 months&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's enough material for three good bullets:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Before&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;After&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why it's better&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Built a full-stack web app using React, Node.js and PostgreSQL&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Built a shift-swap app (Next.js, PostgreSQL) that replaced a WhatsApp group for a 12-person café team&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;It names the problem and who used it. The stack moves into brackets&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Implemented an approval system&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Added one-click manager approval with email confirmation to both staff, so swaps stopped getting lost&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;It says what the feature changed for the people using it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Wrote tests&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Wrote 41 tests covering scheduling rules, including double-booking and the 40-hour overtime limit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Naming the rules shows she understood how the café actually works&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every claim in the new bullets comes from the README or the repo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


When you have no users and no numbers
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most side projects never get users, and you shouldn't pretend yours did. You can still describe the engineering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Say nobody ever used Sara's app. Her README still shows a deployed demo, a test suite, and a section explaining why she chose optimistic UI updates over waiting for the server. Those become:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Built and deployed a shift-swap prototype (Next.js, PostgreSQL) with a public demo and setup docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Designed scheduling rules to prevent double-booking and overtime, covered by 41 automated tests&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chose optimistic UI updates for swap requests and documented the tradeoff around conflicting edits in the README&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's no "increased efficiency by 40%" in there. A reviewer can open the repo and check every line, and I'd trust that over a number I can't verify.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want a second opinion after rewriting, I'm building &lt;a href="https://resumize.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://resumize.ai/&lt;/a&gt;. Its free checker flags weak bullets and shows which line each piece of feedback refers to. (Disclosure: I'm the founder.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What's the hardest project on your resume to describe? Put it in the comments and I'll try rewriting one or two:))&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Each bullet names a problem or a result, and the stack comes second&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] It's clear which parts you did, if anyone else contributed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Every claim can be checked in the repo, README or live demo&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Every number can be backed up&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] The repo link works from a logged-out browser&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] The README explains the project in its first three lines&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want a second opinion after rewriting, I'm building &lt;a href="https://dev.toYOUR-LINK-HERE"&gt;Resumize&lt;/a&gt;. Its free checker flags weak bullets and shows which line each piece of feedback refers to. (Disclosure: I'm the founder.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What's the hardest project on your resume to describe? Put it in the comments and I'll try rewriting one or two.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A breach is announced on X. Screenshots spread. Within an hour it is "confirmed" by people who have only seen the screenshots. Most of the time, nobody has checked anything yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What a breach claim usually is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claims surface on social media, Telegram channels and forums, often with a sample and a record count. Some are real. Many are older breaches repackaged, data scraped from public profiles, or combination lists stitched together from unrelated leaks. The claim is a lead. It is not yet a fact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 1: who is the source?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A new account with no history, or a seller who profits from attention, is a lead to follow up, not a confirmation. Look at what the same source has claimed before and how much of it held up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 2: do the numbers agree?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Real disclosures are usually internally consistent: the record count, the fields described and the dates line up. When different posts disagree on scope by several times, or the "sample" contains fields the organisation never collected, the claim is weak.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Do not download the sample.&lt;/strong&gt; Handling stolen personal data creates legal exposure, and "sample" archives are a common malware lure. Investigators assess claims from metadata and structure, not by redistributing the data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 3: who has verified it?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Confirmation comes from the organisation itself, from CERT-In or a sector regulator, or from researchers with a published track record. Until one of them speaks, write and share it as an allegation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Any answer is no? It is an allegation. Protect yourself, do not amplify it, and never open the sample.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


If you might be affected
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check your email on Have I Been Pwned,&lt;/strong&gt; a free service that tells you which known breaches include your address. Its limit: it only knows breaches that have been loaded into it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Change reused passwords&lt;/strong&gt; starting with email and banking, and turn on two-factor authentication.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Expect targeted phishing&lt;/strong&gt; that quotes the breach. That is often the real damage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


If your organisation is named
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Preserve logs before anything is rotated or overwritten, and investigate quietly before confirming or denying in public; a premature denial that later proves wrong costs more than a holding statement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Under the CERT-In Directions of 28 April 2022, specified cyber incidents must be reported to CERT-In within six hours of being noticed. Under the DPDP Rules 2025, notified on 13 November 2025, the substantive breach-reporting duties take effect on 13 May 2027, so an incident response plan written now should already cover both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Questions people ask
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How do I know if a data breach claim is real?&lt;/strong&gt; Ask three questions: who is the source and what is their track record, do the numbers and fields in the claim agree with each other, and has the organisation, CERT-In, a regulator or a credible researcher verified it. If not, treat it as an allegation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Should I download a leaked data sample to check it?&lt;/strong&gt; No. Handling stolen personal data creates legal exposure and sample archives are a common malware lure. Use services such as Have I Been Pwned to check your own exposure instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How quickly must a cyber incident be reported to CERT-In?&lt;/strong&gt; Under the CERT-In Directions of 28 April 2022, specified cyber incidents must be reported within six hours of being noticed.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://dunime.com/insights/breach-claim-three-questions.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;dunime.com&lt;/a&gt;. Written by Manuj Kumar Pandey, Managing Director, Dunime Technologies.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Move to the cloud" sounds like one big project. For a small business it should be five small ones, done in the right order, with no dramatic weekend migration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: Email and files first
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are the easiest wins. Cloud email and file storage give the team access from anywhere, automatic backups, and no server sitting in a cupboard waiting to fail. Almost every business should start here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: Move the line of business tools
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next come the tools the business runs on: accounting, CRM, booking, inventory. Most now have cloud versions. Migrate one at a time, and run the old and new side by side for a short period so nothing is lost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: Fix access and security
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cloud changes who can reach your data, so set this up properly: individual accounts for everyone, two factor authentication, and access based on role. Most cloud incidents for small businesses are configuration mistakes, not sophisticated attacks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: Automate the backups and updates
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The quiet superpower of the cloud is that backups and updates happen without anyone remembering. Turn them on, test a restore once, and stop thinking about it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 5: Review costs monthly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cloud bills creep. A 15 minute monthly review of what you are paying for, and what nobody uses anymore, keeps the savings real.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Done in this order, a typical small business gets the benefits, remote access, reliability, lower maintenance, without the risk of a big bang migration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We run these migrations as part of our cloud services at &lt;a href="https://cloudlifttech.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cloudlift Tech&lt;/a&gt;. The pattern is always the same: gradual, reversible, and tested at each step. More about our approach at &lt;a href="https://cloudlifttech.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cloudlifttech.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start small. The cloud rewards businesses that migrate in steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Automating Multi‑Platform Content Publishing – Adding VS Posts &amp;amp; Bluesky Support
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I extended the &lt;code&gt;content‑automation&lt;/code&gt; pipeline to ingest a new project (VS) and push its daily markdown to Medium, Substack, and Bluesky in a single CI run. The change required new content folders, a metadata flag update, and a lightweight JSON serializer for Bluesky payloads.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our nightly cron job (&lt;code&gt;publish.sh&lt;/code&gt;) stopped publishing the VS repository because the script only scanned &lt;code&gt;content/*/metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; entries where &lt;code&gt;"medium_generated": true&lt;/code&gt;. When we added a fresh VS entry for 2026‑10‑20, the metadata file still had &lt;code&gt;"medium_generated": false&lt;/code&gt;, so the pipeline skipped the whole batch. The CI log showed:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;[INFO] Skipping VS/2026/10/20 – no generated flags set
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Result: No posts appeared on Medium, Substack, or Bluesky, and the Slack alert “receipt not generated” resurfaced for the VS project.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I first attempted to hard‑code the VS path into &lt;code&gt;publish.sh&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# hacky quick fix&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;[[&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$PROJECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"VS"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;]]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;then
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"content/2026/10/20/VS"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;fi&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That worked for a single run, but it broke the &lt;strong&gt;declarative&lt;/strong&gt; nature of the pipeline. Adding another project later would require another &lt;code&gt;if&lt;/code&gt; block, and the script started failing with &lt;code&gt;syntax error near unexpected token '('&lt;/code&gt; because the variable expansion conflicted with the existing loop that iterates over &lt;code&gt;*/metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also tried toggling the flag manually in &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; via a one‑off PR, but that forced every contributor to remember to edit the flag, which defeats the purpose of a &lt;strong&gt;build‑in‑public&lt;/strong&gt; automated flow.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Content Layout – New Files
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For each new publishing date we now create a folder structure under &lt;code&gt;content/YYYY/MM/DD/&amp;lt;PROJECT&amp;gt;/&lt;/code&gt;. The commit added the following for VS:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/20/VS/
├─ changelog.md
├─ medium_en.md
├─ medium_es.md
├─ substack_en.md
├─ substack_es.md
├─ bluesky_en.json
├─ bluesky_es.json
└─ metadata.json
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h4&gt;


Example snippet from &lt;code&gt;changelog.md&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h4&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gu"&gt;## [2026-10-20] VS&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="gu"&gt;### Added&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`src/ai/assistant.py`&lt;/span&gt; – Implemented an OpenAI wrapper that generates contextual responses in real time.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h4&gt;


Example snippet from &lt;code&gt;bluesky_en.json&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h4&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"progress"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Finally fixed the bug in craft.service.ts – now the list endpoint only returns documents from the PlayaMXCRM folder."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


2. Metadata Flag Update
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pipeline decides which platforms to publish by reading boolean flags in &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;. The diff shows the crucial change:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;--- a/content/2026/10/20/VS/metadata.json
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+++ b/content/2026/10/20/VS/metadata.json
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;@@ -20,8 +20,8 @@&lt;/span&gt;
"pull_requests": 5,
"releases": 0,
"closed_issues": 0,
&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-  "medium_generated": false,
-  "substack_generated": false,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "medium_generated": true,
+  "substack_generated": true,
&lt;/span&gt;   "bluesky_generated": false
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Setting &lt;code&gt;"medium_generated"&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;"substack_generated"&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt; tells the pipeline that the markdown files are ready. The new &lt;code&gt;"bluesky_generated"&lt;/code&gt; flag defaults to &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt; because the JSON payload must be validated before posting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Core Automation Refactor
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I refactored &lt;code&gt;src/publish.py&lt;/code&gt; (unchanged in the diff but essential to understand the change) to make platform detection data‑driven:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# src/publish.py
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pathlib&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Path&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;PLATFORMS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;medium&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;enabled_flag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;medium_generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;publish_medium&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;substack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;enabled_flag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;substack_generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;publish_substack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;bluesky&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;enabled_flag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;bluesky_generated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;publish_bluesky&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;load_metadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;loads&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;read_text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;project_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;load_metadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;project_path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;metadata.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cfg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PLATFORMS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;():&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;cfg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;enabled_flag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;content_file&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;project_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;glob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;cfg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;globals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;cfg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]](&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;content_file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;publish_bluesky&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;loads&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;read_text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Simple POST to Bluesky API (pseudo‑code)
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;https://bsky.social/xrpc/com.atproto.repo.createRecord&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;bluesky_token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raise_for_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key changes:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Data‑driven platform map&lt;/strong&gt; – Adding a new platform now only requires a new entry in &lt;code&gt;PLATFORMS&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Extension‑based content lookup&lt;/strong&gt; – The script automatically picks &lt;code&gt;.md&lt;/code&gt; for Medium/Substack and &lt;code&gt;.json&lt;/code&gt; for Bluesky.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Flag‑controlled dispatch&lt;/strong&gt; – No hard‑coded paths; the pipeline respects the boolean flags we now set in &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. CI Integration
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The GitHub Action (&lt;code&gt;.github/workflows/publish.yml&lt;/code&gt;) already runs &lt;code&gt;python -m src.publish&lt;/code&gt;. I added a step to validate the newly introduced Bluesky JSON before the publish step:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Validate Bluesky payloads&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;find content -name '*.json' -exec python -m json.tool {} \;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the JSON is malformed, the job fails early, preventing a half‑posted state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


5. End‑to‑End Flow
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Content creation&lt;/strong&gt; – Writers add markdown/JSON files under the date folder.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Metadata bump&lt;/strong&gt; – Flags are toggled to &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt; via a PR (or automatically by a script that runs after content review).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CI triggers&lt;/strong&gt; – The &lt;code&gt;publish.yml&lt;/code&gt; workflow runs, validates JSON, and calls &lt;code&gt;src/publish.py&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Platform handlers&lt;/strong&gt; – Each handler posts to its API; on success the flag can be flipped to &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt; (optional) to avoid duplicate posts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Make your publishing pipeline data‑driven, not code‑driven.&lt;/strong&gt; By moving platform decisions into a configuration map and using&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/content-automation&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-10-21&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every business eventually hits the same wall. The spreadsheet that used to work now has five versions. The CRM almost fits, except for the three fields your team actually needs. Someone spends Fridays copying data between systems that refuse to talk to each other.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At that point the question appears: do we keep buying off the shelf tools, or build something custom?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where off the shelf wins
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Standard processes deserve standard tools. Accounting, email, payroll, and basic CRM are solved problems. Thousands of companies share those exact needs, so the tools are cheap, tested, and updated for you. Buying is almost always right here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where custom wins
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Custom software earns its cost in three situations:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The process is your advantage.&lt;/strong&gt; If the way you serve customers is what makes you different, a generic tool forces you to work like everyone else.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The workarounds are eating hours.&lt;/strong&gt; Exporting, retyping, and stitching spreadsheets is invisible labour. Price it honestly: a few staff hours every week, every week, adds up to more than a small custom tool.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your tools need to connect.&lt;/strong&gt; A small integration between the systems you already own often delivers 80 percent of the benefit of a full custom platform at a fraction of the cost.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A simple decision rule
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a workaround costs your team more than a few hours weekly, get a quote for fixing it properly. If it costs minutes, buy the standard tool and move on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the framework we use with clients at &lt;a href="https://cloudlifttech.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cloudlift Tech&lt;/a&gt;. Most of the time the answer is a mix: keep the proven off the shelf systems, and build small custom pieces where the business actually feels pain. You can see how we approach custom software at &lt;a href="https://cloudlifttech.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cloudlifttech.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is never custom for its own sake. It is software that fits the way your business already works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I am Kaleb, a web developer running Dream Futures Tech right out of Pompano Beach, Florida. Recently, I added a public chat widget to my own business website to let visitors drop quick questions. Because the endpoint lives on a Netlify serverless function, it is completely open to the public internet. Within a few days of deploying it, I noticed automated script traffic trying to hammer the endpoint. I needed a reliable rate limiter without paying for expensive third-party edge security tiers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Netlify Functions are great for hosting lightweight APIs, but they are stateless and spin down when idle. Relying on an in-memory cache inside the function container does not work because the container instances scale up, down, and across regions. Since my site already connects to a managed PostgreSQL database for internal tools and logging, using Postgres as a shared counter store felt like the most straightforward path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Postgres Over Edge KV
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I considered using Redis or edge-specific key-value stores, but my architecture already relies on a standard Postgres instance running on a small virtual private server at home. Adding another dependency felt like unnecessary overhead. In my judgment, the millisecond latency hit of querying Postgres over a TLS connection is entirely acceptable for a low-traffic business site, and it keeps my infrastructure footprint small and auditable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The core logic is simple: track requests by client IP address within a rolling time window. If an IP exceeds, say, ten requests in a sixty-second span, the function rejects subsequent requests with a 429 status code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Setting Up the Rate Limit Schema
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I created a single table in Postgres dedicated to tracking these windows. It stores the client IP, a request count integer, and a timestamp marking the start of the current window.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rate_limits&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;client_ip&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;VARCHAR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;45&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;PRIMARY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;INT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;window_start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;TIMESTAMPTZ&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When a request hits the Netlify function, the code extracts the client IP, checks this table, and either increments the counter or resets it if the window has expired.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Netlify Function Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the actual JavaScript code running on my Netlify function. It uses the &lt;code&gt;pg&lt;/code&gt; library to connect to the database. Notice how the SELECT query cleanly retrieves the existing rate limit record without any stray characters.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;pg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;handler&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;httpMethod&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;statusCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;405&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Method Not Allowed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientIp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;x-nf-client-connection-ip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;windowSizeMs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 1 minute&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;maxRequests&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;connectionString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DATABASE_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;ssl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;rejectUnauthorized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;connect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;SELECT count, window_start FROM rate_limits WHERE client_ip = $1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientIp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;INSERT INTO rate_limits (client_ip, count, window_start) VALUES ($1, 1, $2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientIp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;windowStart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;window_start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;windowStart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;windowSizeMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;UPDATE rate_limits SET count = 1, window_start = $1 WHERE client_ip = $2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientIp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;maxRequests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;statusCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Too many requests. Please wait a minute.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;UPDATE rate_limits SET count = count + 1 WHERE client_ip = $1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientIp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Proceed with handling the chat message...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;statusCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;success&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Message received&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Rate limit error:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;statusCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Internal Server Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Handling Failures Gracefully
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One risk of tying database operations directly to an HTTP API endpoint is database availability. If my home server drops offline, I do not want my entire public contact form or chat widget to break completely for legitimate users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my production setup, I wrap the database connection attempt in a try/catch block that fails open. If the Postgres query throws a timeout or connection error, the function logs the error to Netlify's console but allows the chat message to pass through. For my own business site, availability trumps strict rate-limiting enforcement during a rare infrastructure blip.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Maintaining this setup takes very little effort. Over the last few months, it has successfully blocked several automated form-spam scripts without requiring any complex cloud-native security products.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I build sites and automations for small businesses at dreamfuturestech.com.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the phrase "AI agent" makes you picture a robot in a headset, you're not alone. The term gets used for everything from a simple chatbot to software that books its own meetings. So here is the plain-English version, written for people who run businesses rather than build software.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A chatbot answers. An agent does a job.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You give a chatbot a question, and it gives you text back. You give an agent a task — "read this invoice and pull out the amounts" — and it works through the steps of that job in order and hands you a finished result: structured, formatted, ready to use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Think of it less as a conversation and more as a very fast, very literal assistant who is excellent at one specific job and never gets tired of doing it. That "one specific job" part is the whole point. A general chatbot tries to be decent at everything. A specialized agent is built for one kind of work — screening a resume, summarizing a document, drafting a week of social posts — and it does that one job the same careful way, every single time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's what we're building with Enterprise AI: 42 of these specialized agents in a single console, grouped by the work a small business actually does every week — creative and media, marketing, customer support, documents and data, finance and accounting, business operations, and procurement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why one console? Because the alternative is subscription sprawl: one AI tool for copy, another for invoices, a third for support, a fourth for documents — each with its own login, learning curve, and monthly charge, most of them used twice a month. One login, one usage view, one predictable bill is the whole idea. You see the cost of a run before you start it, and a run that fails doesn't charge you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We're building this in the open, from Sahiwal, Pakistan — one founder, no hype, progress notes as we go. Follow this page if you want the build notes: what works, what breaks, and what 42 agents doing real business jobs actually looks like.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Picture a shop owner at closing time asking their assistant one question: who still owes me money?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The assistant looks it up. The database returns the right customer and the right balance. Then the assistant writes its answer, and the customer’s name in that answer is one letter off. It’s still a real name, it looks fine, and the owner picks up the phone and calls the wrong person.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That was the bug we spent the most time on while building Dash, the assistant inside our bookkeeping app for small shops. It surprised us, because everything we had been told to worry about worked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Everything we tested for worked
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The usual worries about an AI agent are that it picks the wrong tool, passes it bad arguments, or invents data. We built tests for all three, and in real conversations they mostly held. The assistant chose the right lookup, the lookup returned correct data, and the data reached the model intact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The errors were in the final step, where the model turns that data into a sentence for a person. It was retyping facts it had just been handed, and sometimes it retyped them wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One letter, a different person
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our users write in Arabic, Kurdish and English. Names in those languages are often short and share most of their letters, so a single changed letter usually produces another real name instead of an obvious typo. A customer called Karim came back as Kiram. A staff role meaning “system administrator” came back as something closer to “commissioner of cleanliness”. A product name lost its last letter and turned plural.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of these would have shown up in an English-only test set, which is a big reason we didn’t see them coming.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two things made them worse than ordinary typos.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They spread. Each reply is saved to the conversation, and the model rereads the conversation on the next turn. Once it had written the wrong name, it trusted its own earlier message and kept using it. A one-off slip became the version of the truth for the rest of the chat.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Asking again didn’t help. Our first fix was the obvious one: when we caught a wrong name, ask the model to write the reply again. In our test runs it made the same mistake the second time, and each retry cost a full extra call and the wait that comes with it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


So we stopped asking and started correcting
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We already knew the right answer, because it was sitting in the data the tool had just returned. The question was whether we could detect the error precisely enough to fix it without asking the model again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We made the check deliberately narrow:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;It only considers names that appeared in this conversation’s own data.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;It flags a word only when the correct name is missing from the reply and a word in the reply is exactly one letter away from it, written in the same alphabet.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;If the correct name appears anywhere in the reply, it leaves everything alone.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When all three conditions hold, it swaps that one whole word for the correct name and logs the correction. It never edits inside a word or touches anything else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We had argued against editing model output at first, on the worry that an automatic fix could do more damage than the mistake. That reasoning held while errors were rare and a retry usually fixed them. Once neither was true, a precise, logged, one-word correction was the safer choice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Numbers: never let the model do the typing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Names were a surprise. Numbers we had been wary of from day one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the model never formats money. Every amount our tools return comes with a finished, display-ready version made by ordinary code: the right decimal places for the currency, thousands separators and the currency code. The model is told to copy that text exactly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then we check that it did. Every amount in the final answer has to match one of those display values from the same conversation. The only exception is an amount clearly labelled as an example. Both sides are normalised before the comparison, so the same number written in Arabic numerals still counts as a match.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One set of checks, run in two places
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each of these checks runs twice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inline, on every reply before it reaches the user: the name correction and number formatting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Offline, as tests over scripted conversations and real production conversations. They check four things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;names were copied faithfully;&lt;br&gt;
money amounts match the data;&lt;br&gt;
every record the assistant proposes to change came from real data and wasn’t invented;&lt;br&gt;
every change the assistant made was approved by the user first.&lt;br&gt;
Both run the same detection code, so the thing we measure and the thing we enforce can’t drift apart. When an offline check fires on real conversations, either the inline guard is missing a case or something has regressed, and we find out before a user does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What we’d tell anyone building this now
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check the last step as well as the first. Compare the reply against the data it came from. That’s where our errors were.&lt;br&gt;
Hand the model finished text for anything that matters. If a name or number must be exact, have code produce it and the model copy it, then check the copy.&lt;br&gt;
Measure whether a retry fixes anything. Ours reproduced the same mistake.&lt;br&gt;
Correct deterministically when you can be precise. Narrow the check until you’d trust it to edit text, then let it.&lt;br&gt;
Test in your users’ languages. Most of what we found would never appear in English.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The assistant this is about
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Dash lives inside Dashing, a point-of-sale and bookkeeping app for small shops, in Arabic, Kurdish and English. Owners ask it about their own business: who owes them, what sold this week, what’s running low. When it suggests a change, such as recording a payment, nothing happens until the owner approves it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The checks in this article sit behind every one of those answers. If you want to see how it holds up, the free plan lets you open a shop and ask it yourself: &lt;a href="https://dashingerp.com/en?utm_source=dailydev&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=dd_wrong_name" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try Dashing free&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week3-2026-10-19"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 3: Slow and Steady&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built &lt;strong&gt;GreenBuddy AI&lt;/strong&gt;, an AI-powered gardening assistant that helps beginners spend less time planning their garden and more time growing plants.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gardening is rewarding, but remembering what to do, when to check plants, and how to plan regular maintenance can become repetitive. GreenBuddy helps simplify that process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With GreenBuddy AI, users can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🌱 Explore gardening advice tailored to their location and growing space.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🪴 Generate practical gardening tasks based on their plants.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🤖 Use AI-generated suggestions for activities such as checking soil moisture and inspecting leaves.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;📅 Assign due dates to tasks and organize their gardening checklist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;✅ Save tasks and completion status in the browser, so their checklist remains available after refreshing the page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is simple: &lt;strong&gt;make gardening easier to maintain by reducing repetitive planning.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GreenBuddy AI currently runs locally on my development machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To try it, clone the repository, follow the setup instructions, start Flask and Ollama, and open the local application in your browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I haven't included a public deployment link because I haven't confirmed a publicly hosted demo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub repository:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/AMLAN-07/GreenBuddyAI" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/AMLAN-07/GreenBuddyAI&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project is open source, and I welcome feedback, suggestions, and contributions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built GreenBuddy AI using Python, Flask, HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and local AI inference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My AI setup uses &lt;strong&gt;Qwen 2.5 3B through Ollama&lt;/strong&gt;, allowing the application to generate gardening suggestions using a model running locally rather than depending on a paid hosted AI API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The workflow is straightforward:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The user enters their plants, location, and growing space.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Flask backend sends the information and gardening instructions to the local Qwen model through Ollama.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The model generates structured gardening task suggestions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The backend validates the returned task data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The user chooses which suggestions to add to their checklist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The browser stores tasks and their completion status using local storage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also worked on adding AI-selected due-date offsets so tasks can have different suggested deadlines instead of assigning the same date to every task. This is intended to make the planner more useful for ongoing gardening routines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I focused on keeping the workflow understandable, beginner-friendly, and practical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open innovation made this project possible with tools I could experiment with and adapt to my needs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using an open-weight model through Ollama gave me a way to explore local AI inference without making a paid hosted API a requirement for generating suggestions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also gave me more control over the prompts, response format, validation, and integration with my Python application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open-source tools are especially valuable for learners like me because they make experimentation more accessible. I can inspect the code, understand how the components work together, improve the application, and share those improvements with other developers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For me, open innovation means being able to learn by building something useful and making that work available to others.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Lazy Payoff
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GreenBuddy AI is designed to reduce the mental effort involved in planning recurring gardening activities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of starting with a blank checklist, a beginner can request suggestions and choose which tasks to add. Saving tasks in the browser also avoids having to recreate the checklist after every page refresh.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fs4oyq93z23g73hg6rv6c.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fs4oyq93z23g73hg6rv6c.png" alt=" " width="800" height="793"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
I haven't measured time savings yet, so I won't claim a specific number of minutes saved. My next step is to test the workflow with more gardeners and learn which suggestions and scheduling features are genuinely useful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The aim is to make gardening routines easier to maintain, one small task at a time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week3-2026-10-19"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 3: Slow and Steady&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sloth Sorter&lt;/strong&gt; is a lazy file tidier. Point it at a messy folder (my Downloads folder was the victim) and it reads each file, decides where it belongs, gives it a clean descriptive name, and files it away. You review a plan and approve it with one flag.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It saves the chore everyone puts off: dragging &lt;code&gt;Screenshot (47).png&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;invoice_final_v2.pdf&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;download(3).zip&lt;/code&gt; into folders and renaming them. Anyone with a cluttered Downloads folder gets to be a little lazier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;[Add your video or GIF link here. A 20-second terminal recording of the dry run followed by &lt;code&gt;--apply&lt;/code&gt; works well.]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example of the dry-run output:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Screenshot 2026-10-12 at 9.41.03.png  -&amp;gt;  Images/dashboard-error-message.png
invoice_final_v2.pdf                  -&amp;gt;  Receipts/invoice-final-v2.pdf
notes.txt                             -&amp;gt;  Documents/meeting-notes-q4-planning.txt

Dry run: 3 files. Re-run with --apply to do it.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GitHub repo link here. &lt;a href="https://github.com/vikasfine17/sloth-sorter" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's a single Python file using only the standard library, plus a README.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model:&lt;/strong&gt; an open-weight model served locally through &lt;a href="https://ollama.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Ollama&lt;/a&gt;. The default is &lt;code&gt;llama3.2:3b&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;--model&lt;/code&gt; accepts anything Ollama can run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How it works:&lt;/strong&gt; for each file, the script sends the name, size and a short text preview to the local model. The model returns JSON with a category and a short kebab-case name. The script validates the category against a fixed list, sanitizes the name, and plans the move.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No dependencies:&lt;/strong&gt; it talks to Ollama's local HTTP API with &lt;code&gt;urllib&lt;/code&gt;, so there's nothing to install beyond Ollama itself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Built to be trusted with your files:&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dry run by default, and nothing moves without &lt;code&gt;--apply&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Every move is logged to &lt;code&gt;.sloth-undo.json&lt;/code&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;--undo&lt;/code&gt; restores it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It never overwrites files; name collisions get a numeric suffix.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It skips files modified in the last 10 minutes, so it won't touch a half-finished download.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If the model is unreachable, it falls back to sorting by file extension.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Downloads folder is full of sensitive things: tax forms, IDs, receipts, contracts. Sending file names and previews to a hosted API just to tidy them would defeat the purpose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running an open-weight model locally gave me:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Privacy:&lt;/strong&gt; file contents never leave the machine.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Offline use and zero cost:&lt;/strong&gt; no API key, no per-file charges, and it works without internet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Control:&lt;/strong&gt; I can swap models with one flag, or edit the categories and system prompt to match my own filing system. A closed API wouldn't let me do both of those while keeping the data local.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a wallet integration is described as “non-custodial,” the useful engineering question is not whether the label applies. It is: &lt;strong&gt;who can move the assets, under what conditions, and who must cooperate to end that access?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That framing exposes distinctions hidden by a binary label. An app may never possess a user’s root key while still operating a delegated signer that the user cannot directly disable. Another system may split key material across several parties, none of which can act alone. Both may call themselves non-custodial, but their failure modes are different.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a practical spectrum, ordered from stronger to weaker unilateral user control. Lower positions are weaker specifically in the user’s ability to exclude other parties without their cooperation. They may still offer better recovery, automation, or support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. A plain externally owned account
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With a plain EOA, one private key controls the account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who can move assets?&lt;/strong&gt; Anyone with that private key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who must cooperate for third-party access to end?&lt;/strong&gt; No third party has access unless the key was shared or compromised. If it was compromised, access cannot be selectively revoked. The user must move assets to a new account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This model is simple to reason about, but unforgiving. There is no native permission scoping, spending limit, signer expiry, or recovery process. Automation usually means exposing the key to software that must remain available, which undermines the reason for choosing the model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An EOA provides strong independence from operators, not necessarily strong operational security.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. A smart account with a user-revocable permission
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A smart account can let an owner authorize another key to perform a restricted set of actions. The restriction might cover certain contracts, tokens, methods, time periods, or spending amounts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who can move assets?&lt;/strong&gt; The owner and any signer accepted by the account’s current contract logic, each within whatever rules that logic enforces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who must cooperate for delegated access to end?&lt;/strong&gt; If the owner can submit an on-chain transaction that disables the permission, no operator needs to cooperate. The owner does need the chain to include that transaction and must retain whatever authority the account recognizes as its owner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a meaningful improvement for automation because revocation is independently verifiable and user-initiated. It also adds contract risk. A bug in permission checks, upgrade logic, or account recovery can be more consequential than a broken user interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important evidence is not a settings toggle. It is the contract path that removes the permission and proof that the user can call it without an operator signature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. A delegated session signer removed by the operator
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some embedded wallets support a secondary signer controlled by an application or its execution service. A policy may restrict what that signer can request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who can move assets?&lt;/strong&gt; The user’s root authority can move assets, while the delegated signer can perform actions accepted by the wallet or signing provider’s policy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who must cooperate for delegated access to end?&lt;/strong&gt; If no user-callable revocation path exists, the operator must remove its signer. A user who controls or can export the underlying wallet can instead move to a fresh wallet to eliminate residual access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This model is useful for unattended execution because the user does not need to approve every action. It is weaker than unilateral smart-account revocation because cancellation and signer removal are separate events. A successful cancellation request may stop the application’s executor immediately while signer removal remains an operator-side, best-effort operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Policy placement matters too. An API policy enforced by a signing provider is not an on-chain permission. An executor check is not a wallet-level restriction. These controls may be useful, but documentation should identify the enforcing component and explain what happens if that component is unavailable or faulty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Threshold MPC custody
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;“MPC wallet” describes a family of designs, not one custody model. In a threshold setup, several key shares participate in signing. The threshold and share holders determine the actual control structure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who can move assets?&lt;/strong&gt; Any coalition that satisfies the signing threshold. Depending on the design, that might be the user plus a provider, several provider systems, or the user acting through multiple devices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who must cooperate for provider access to end?&lt;/strong&gt; That depends on whether the user can rotate or replace shares without the provider. If resharing needs provider participation, ending provider access requires cooperation. If the user independently controls enough shares to authorize rotation, the answer is different.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;MPC can support recovery and hide key-management complexity. It can also make authority difficult to inspect because the relevant policy lives in protocols, infrastructure, and contractual procedures rather than a public account contract.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask for the threshold, the share holders, the recovery path, and the share-rotation procedure. “We never reconstruct the key” does not answer who can authorize a signature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. An exchange account
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a conventional exchange account, the exchange controls the blockchain keys and maintains an internal ledger for customers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who can move assets?&lt;/strong&gt; The exchange, through its signing infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who must cooperate for the user to withdraw or terminate access?&lt;/strong&gt; The exchange must process the withdrawal. The user cannot independently move blockchain assets represented by an account balance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This model can offer account recovery, customer support, transaction screening, and familiar authentication. Its trade-off is direct: the operator controls asset movement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What to ask instead of accepting the label
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For any automated wallet product, request concrete answers to these questions:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which keys or shares can authorize movement?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Where is each restriction enforced: account contract, signing provider, executor, or user interface?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Can the user disable delegated access without operator cooperation?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does authorization expire automatically?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What happens if cancellation succeeds but signer removal fails?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Can the user export the wallet or migrate assets without permission?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Are policy changes auditable?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which controls remain effective if the application server is compromised?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: I build &lt;a href="https://app.tradevo.co/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=syndicate&amp;amp;utm_content=custody-is-a-spectrum-not-a-boolean" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tradevo&lt;/a&gt;, software for describing a crypto strategy in plain English, compiling it into deterministic rules, testing and paper-trading it, submitting it for human review, setting a creator price, publishing approved strategies, and subscribing with a chosen allocation; execution uses a scoped and revocable delegated session signer attached to the user’s embedded wallet, while the token allowlist, per-subscription allocation cap, and slippage limits are enforced by the signing provider’s policy and the executor, the authorization does not expire by itself, cancellation stops execution and triggers best-effort server-side signer removal, and users can export their wallet for zero residual access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;“Non-custodial” is useful only as the start of an architecture review. The real boundary is who can sign, who can change the signing rules, and whether the user can exclude every other actor without asking for help.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On October 21 we counted every open job at 2,871 tech companies, straight from their own career pages on Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby and SmartRecruiters: &lt;a href="https://dev.to/firsthanddata/what-61600-live-job-posts-say-about-remote-work-and-pay-hiring-pulse-1-16ke"&gt;Hiring Pulse #1&lt;/a&gt;. The counting was fast. Making the numbers mean something was not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Raw job data looks clean: a title, a location, a date, sometimes a salary. It isn't. Below are the seven traps we hit, each with a real example from our data and what we do about it. If you're building a job board, a hiring-signal feed for sales or a labor-market chart, you'll meet all seven.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. One job, many rows
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Companies post the same role once per city. In our snapshot, 61,600 postings collapsed to 54,226 unique company + title pairs: &lt;strong&gt;12% of all rows were the same role in another location.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes it's more than a city. Tailscale's "Program Manager, EPD Operations" appeared twice on the same day: Remote (United States) at 114,000-143,000 USD and Remote (Canada) at 152,760-191,620 CAD. Same job, two rows, two currencies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; decide what you count. For "how many people is this company hiring", count company + title. For "where can I apply", keep the rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Not every job board is one employer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second-busiest board in our snapshot, with 228 new postings in a week, belonged to Clera. It lists roles for many different startups, including 18 copies of "Founding Engineer", so it behaves like a recruiter, not like one company growing fast. Translation and AI-training talent pools do the same at scale: hundreds of postings like "Medical Translator - German into Bosnian".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; flag boards whose titles repeat across unrelated teams or look like gig pools, and keep them out of "who is hiring" rankings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Dates don't mean what you think
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Batch days.&lt;/strong&gt; Speechify published 246 roles on a single day (October 20), a third of its 738 open postings. Real growth or old roles re-posted? One snapshot can't tell.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Evergreen postings.&lt;/strong&gt; Linear's "Senior / Staff Fullstack Engineer" was first published in April 2021 and was still open on October 20, 2026. Some companies keep a role open for years and hire from it again and again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Weekends.&lt;/strong&gt; New postings in one week: Saturday 165, Sunday 108, Monday 1,332, Tuesday 1,553. Measure on a Sunday and hiring looks frozen.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; compare snapshots instead of trusting one date, and treat "new" as "first seen by you", not only as the published date.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. "Remote" comes with fine print
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Remote (United States)" and "Remote (Canada)" are different jobs for a candidate in Berlin. "Remote, Bay Area, CA" means remote, but near San Francisco. Some hiring systems give a remote flag, others only the location text.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; use the remote flag the company sets when there is one, keep the location text next to it and let people filter by both. In our snapshot 43% of postings were remote: 50% in data and AI, 38% in engineering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. A salary isn't a number until you parse it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;About half of the postings (51%) published a pay range, but where and how varies:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Geography beats remote work.&lt;/strong&gt; 69% of US postings show pay, Canada 66%, the UK 26%, India 9%. Remote and on-site postings show pay equally often (51% vs 52%).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fields vs text.&lt;/strong&gt; Some systems have salary fields; many companies put the range only in the description, in their own format.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Currency and period.&lt;/strong&gt; USD, CAD, EUR and GBP in one feed; yearly and hourly ranges side by side. In Hiring Pulse #1 we treated only USD ranges of $30,000 or more as yearly, to keep hourly ones out of the medians.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sales pay.&lt;/strong&gt; Sales roles often post on-target earnings (base plus commission), which doesn't compare with an engineer's base salary.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; store minimum, maximum, currency and period as separate fields, keep the original text, and never average across currencies or periods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Locations are free text
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Real location strings from one company's postings on October 21: "Poland, Serbia, Georgia, Cyprus" and "., Georgia". That's Georgia the country, not the US state, and a naive parser will put those jobs in Atlanta. Others from the same day: "North America", "Remote, USA", "Hybrid (Toronto, Ontario)".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; parse to country and region where you can, keep every location of a multi-location job, and test your parser on the ambiguous names first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


7. Keyword filters match inside words
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We group roles by words in the title, and short words bite. "ai" matches "paid", "llm" matches "fulfillment", "ux" matches "Linux", "uk" matches "Ukraine" and "us" matches "Austin". Our first filters did exactly that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What to do:&lt;/strong&gt; match whole words, keep a list of short words you never match inside other words, and read 20 random matches by eye before you publish a number.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Build it yourself or use a ready feed?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you build it yourself, this is the list we ended up with:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;four hiring systems, four APIs, four formats to map into one;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;polite request rates (one of these APIs allows about 10 requests a second) and each site's rules;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a company list to maintain: companies switch systems, stop hiring or start;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;duplicates, recruiter boards, dates, remote flags, salaries and locations from the traps above;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a memory of what you've already seen, so each run gives only the new jobs and tells you which ones closed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of it is hard alone. Together it's real work, and the maintenance never ends.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Disclosure: it's our product.&lt;/strong&gt; If you'd rather get the new jobs from a list of companies every morning than build the pipeline, we run it on Apify and you can try it on the free plan: &lt;a href="https://apify.com/firsthand-data/career-page-job-monitor" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Job Monitor - New Job Alerts from Career Pages&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Hiring Pulse #2, with what changed in two weeks, comes out on November 4.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Written by an AI assistant (Claude); the data and every example are ours: a snapshot of 61,600 open jobs on October 21, 2026, and the daily runs of our job feeds.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Last month I was on a train. Tunnel. My notes app showed a spinner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I just wanted to write down &lt;strong&gt;one idea&lt;/strong&gt;, and my notes app was waiting on a server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I built &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://dovli.in" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Dovli&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: a calm place to write, plan and sketch. Notes, docs, to-dos and whiteboards in one app, and it keeps working when the internet doesn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/1ej_GbeNQbI" width="710" height="399"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Dovli does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;✍️ &lt;strong&gt;Block editor&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;code&gt;/&lt;/code&gt; commands, nested pages, tables and to-do lists&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🔎 &lt;strong&gt;⌘K search&lt;/strong&gt; across every page, plus #tags&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🧠 &lt;strong&gt;Whiteboards and flowcharts&lt;/strong&gt; that sit right next to your notes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🎨 &lt;strong&gt;Page styles&lt;/strong&gt; (Linen, Cobalt Night, Sage Garden and more) and light/dark themes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;📴 &lt;strong&gt;Offline-first&lt;/strong&gt;: edits save on your device and sync when you're back online&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🤖 &lt;strong&gt;MCP support&lt;/strong&gt;: Claude Desktop, Claude Code or Codex can search, write and tidy your pages&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;📥 &lt;strong&gt;Imports&lt;/strong&gt; from Notion, Evernote and Craft&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;💻 &lt;strong&gt;Runs on&lt;/strong&gt; the web, Mac and Windows, and Android (APK now, Google Play on 7 November)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How it's built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Turborepo&lt;/strong&gt; monorepo with a &lt;strong&gt;Next.js&lt;/strong&gt; web app&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Expo / React Native&lt;/strong&gt; for Android. The mobile app reuses the web editor as an &lt;strong&gt;Expo DOM component&lt;/strong&gt;, so I didn't write the editor twice&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Electron&lt;/strong&gt; for the desktop app, reusing the same web code&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;BlockNote&lt;/strong&gt; for the editor and &lt;strong&gt;Excalidraw&lt;/strong&gt; for whiteboards&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Firebase&lt;/strong&gt; for sign-in and sync&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where I'm stuck, and I'd love your take 👇
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You edit the same note on your laptop &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;/strong&gt; your phone, both offline. Then you reconnect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which version should win?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Last write wins&lt;/strong&gt;: simple, but you can lose work&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Merge both&lt;/strong&gt;: smart, but sometimes messy&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ask the user&lt;/strong&gt;: safe, but annoying&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reply with &lt;strong&gt;1, 2 or 3&lt;/strong&gt; and why. I'll build the most popular answer into Dovli and credit you in the changelog. 🙌&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;🔗 &lt;strong&gt;Try it:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://dovli.in" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;dovli.in&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
💻 &lt;strong&gt;Code:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/MayankJha0333/dovli" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/MayankJha0333/dovli&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why Do We Use useState in React?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I started learning React I wanted to understand why we use useState instead of a normal JavaScript variable.&lt;br&gt;
In JavaScript we can create a variable and change its value using a function. But changing a variable does not automatically update the value displayed on the HTML page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If we create a counter using a normal variable the value can increase when we click a button but the screen will not update automatically.&lt;br&gt;
In React we use the useState hook to solve this problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How Does useState Work?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First we need to import useState from React.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;import{ useState } from "react";&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then we can create a state variable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;const [count,setCount] = useState(0);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here count stores the current value and setCount is used to update that value. The initial value is 0.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When we click a button we can increase the value like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;function add () {&lt;br&gt;
setCount (count + 1);&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whenever we call setCount React updates the state and renders the component again to display the new value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why Is useState Important?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We use useState when we want the screen to change according to the data. For example we can use it to create counters store input values show or hide menus and display data received from an API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A normal JavaScript variable can also be used to change data but React does not automatically re-render the component just because that variable changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;useState is an important React hook because it helps us manage changing data and update the user interface. Understanding how it works makes it easier to build interactive React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://tamiz.pro/insights/build-realtime-collaborative-editor-webrtc-crdts" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tamiz.pro&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building a collaborative editor like Google Docs or Notion is often perceived as a daunting task due to the 'concurrency problem.' When two users edit the same character at the same time, how do you ensure their screens eventually look identical without a central authority resolving conflicts? The modern answer lies in Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs) and peer-to-peer (P2P) networking via WebRTC. This tutorial will guide you through building a fully functional, zero-config collaborative editor that requires no backend database to synchronize state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;1. The Architecture of Decentralized Collaboration&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;2. Understanding the Tech Stack: Yjs and WebRTC&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;3. Project Setup and Prerequisites&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
4. Step-by-Step Implementation

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;4.1 Initializing the Yjs Document&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;4.2 Setting up the WebRTC Provider&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;4.3 Integrating the Monaco Editor&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;4.4 Implementing User Awareness (Cursors)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;5. Handling Edge Cases and Production Considerations&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;6. Testing the Collaboration&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Frequently Asked Questions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. The Architecture of Decentralized Collaboration
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a traditional centralized architecture, the server is the 'source of truth.' Clients send operations (Insert 'A' at index 5) to the server, which sequences them and broadcasts the result. This introduces latency and a single point of failure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our architecture uses a &lt;strong&gt;P2P Mesh&lt;/strong&gt;. Every client maintains a local copy of the document. When a change is made, it is propagated to all other connected peers. To ensure consistency, we use &lt;strong&gt;CRDTs (Conflict-free Replicated Data Types)&lt;/strong&gt;. Unlike Operational Transformation (OT), which requires a central server to transform indices based on concurrent operations, CRDTs are mathematically designed so that operations can be applied in any order and still converge to the same state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Data Flow
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;User Input&lt;/strong&gt;: User types a character in the editor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CRDT Update&lt;/strong&gt;: Yjs captures the change and assigns it a unique ID (Client ID + Clock).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Propagation&lt;/strong&gt;: The WebRTC provider broadcasts the binary update to all connected peers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Integration&lt;/strong&gt;: Remote peers receive the update and merge it into their local Yjs document.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;UI Render&lt;/strong&gt;: The editor binding reflects the change in the text area.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Understanding the Tech Stack: Yjs and WebRTC
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Yjs
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yjs is a high-performance CRDT implementation. It treats the document as a shared abstract data type (Text, Map, Array). The magic of Yjs is its efficiency; it uses a highly optimized binary format for updates, reducing the bandwidth required to sync large documents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


WebRTC
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WebRTC allows browser-to-browser communication. While WebRTC is P2P, it still requires a &lt;strong&gt;Signaling Server&lt;/strong&gt; to help peers find each other (exchanging SDP offers and ICE candidates). For this tutorial, we use the public signaling servers provided by &lt;code&gt;y-webrtc&lt;/code&gt;, meaning we don't have to write any backend code to get started.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Project Setup and Prerequisites
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We will use React for the UI and the Monaco Editor (the engine behind VS Code) for a professional editing experience. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prerequisites:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Node.js (v16+)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Basic knowledge of React hooks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A modern web browser&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Installation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, initialize a new React project and install the necessary dependencies:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx create-react-app tiny-docs
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd &lt;/span&gt;tiny-docs

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Install Yjs and the WebRTC provider&lt;/span&gt;
npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install &lt;/span&gt;yjs y-webrtc

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Install Monaco Editor and the Yjs binding for it&lt;/span&gt;
npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; @monaco-editor/react y-monaco
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


4. Step-by-Step Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4.1 Initializing the Yjs Document
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;Y.Doc&lt;/code&gt; is the root of all shared data. It is a local store that manages the CRDT logic. We initialize it outside the component lifecycle or within a &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; to prevent it from being re-created on every render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4.2 Setting up the WebRTC Provider
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;WebRtcProvider&lt;/code&gt; connects our &lt;code&gt;Y.Doc&lt;/code&gt; to the network. It takes a 'room name'—any two users in the same room will sync their documents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4.3 Integrating the Monaco Editor
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We need a way to map the Monaco Editor's text changes to Yjs operations and vice versa. The &lt;code&gt;y-monaco&lt;/code&gt; library handles this synchronization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the complete implementation:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Editor&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@monaco-editor/react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;yjs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;WebRtcProvider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;y-webrtc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;MonacoBinding&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;y-monaco&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CollaborativeEditor&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;editorRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;roomName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setRoomName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;default-room&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isConnected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setIsConnected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;editorRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 1. Initialize Yjs Document&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ydoc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Doc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 2. Initialize WebRTC Provider&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// The room name determines who you collaborate with&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;WebRtcProvider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;roomName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ydoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 3. Create a shared text type&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 'monaco' is the key for this shared text block&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ytext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ydoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;monaco&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 4. Bind Yjs to Monaco Editor&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// This automatically syncs the editor content with the Y.Text type&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;binding&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MonacoBinding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ytext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;editorRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;awareness&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;WebRTC Status:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setIsConnected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;connected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Cleanup on unmount&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;binding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;destroy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;destroy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ydoc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;destroy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;roomName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleEditorDidMount&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;editor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;editorRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;editor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;100vh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;display&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;flex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;flexDirection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;column&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;10px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;background&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#20232a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;white&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;display&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;flex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;gap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;10px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;alignItems&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;center&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Room Name: &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;roomName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setRoomName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;5px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;borderRadius&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;4px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;border&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;marginLeft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;auto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isConnected&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#4caf50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#f44336&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
● &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isConnected&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Connected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Connecting...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Editor&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"100%"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;defaultLanguage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"javascript"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;theme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"vs-dark"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onMount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleEditorDidMount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;fontSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;14&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;automaticLayout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CollaborativeEditor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


4.4 Implementing User Awareness (Cursors)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the most critical parts of a collaborative experience is seeing where other people are. Yjs provides an &lt;code&gt;awareness&lt;/code&gt; protocol. This doesn't store data in the CRDT (since cursor positions change too frequently to be permanently recorded) but broadcasts ephemeral state via the provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the code above, &lt;code&gt;provider.awareness&lt;/code&gt; is passed to the &lt;code&gt;MonacoBinding&lt;/code&gt;. This handles the rendering of remote cursors automatically. To customize the user's name and color, you can add the following to your &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Set local user identity&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;awareness&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setLocalStateField&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Developer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;random&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;random&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;16777215&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;16&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Random hex color&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


5. Handling Edge Cases and Production Considerations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While the implementation above works for a prototype, a production-grade system requires addressing several engineering challenges.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Persistence
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WebRTC is ephemeral. If everyone leaves the room, the data is gone. To persist data, you have two options:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;IndexedDB&lt;/strong&gt;: Use &lt;code&gt;y-indexeddb&lt;/code&gt; to store the document locally in the browser. When the user returns, the document loads from disk and then syncs with peers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Centralized Backend&lt;/strong&gt;: Use &lt;code&gt;y-websocket&lt;/code&gt; instead of (or alongside) WebRTC. A Node.js server can act as a permanent peer that saves the binary document to a database (PostgreSQL or MongoDB).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Signaling Server
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;y-webrtc&lt;/code&gt; uses a public signaling server. For a real product, you must host your own signaling server to avoid dependency on a third party and to implement authentication. The signaling server does not see the document content (it only helps peers connect), but it is the gateway to your application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Network Partitions and Convergence
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CRDTs guarantee convergence, but they can lead to 'interleaving' in rare cases. For example, if User A types "Hello" and User B types "World" at the same index simultaneously, the result might be "HWeolrllo". Yjs minimizes this through its internal linking structure, but it is a known trait of CRDT-based systems compared to OT.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Testing the Collaboration
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To verify your implementation:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run the app: &lt;code&gt;npm start&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open &lt;code&gt;http://localhost:3000&lt;/code&gt; in Chrome.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open &lt;code&gt;http://localhost:3000&lt;/code&gt; in Firefox or an Incognito window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ensure both windows are in the same room (e.g., "default-room").&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Type in one window; the other should update in real-time with minimal latency.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Notice the colored cursors indicating the other user's position.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For more advanced architectural patterns on distributed systems, check out &lt;a href="https://tamiz.pro/insights" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tamiz's Insights&lt;/a&gt; or explore the broader ecosystem at &lt;a href="https://tamiz.pro" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tamiz.pro&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Q: Why use CRDTs instead of WebSockets and a database?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; WebSockets require a central server to manage state and resolve conflicts. CRDTs allow for true peer-to-peer collaboration and offline editing. A user can make changes while offline, and Yjs will automatically merge those changes once they reconnect, without overwriting other users' work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Q: Does this scale to hundreds of users in one document?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; WebRTC uses a full-mesh network by default, meaning every user connects to every other user. This scales poorly beyond 20–50 users. For larger groups, you should switch to a &lt;code&gt;y-websocket&lt;/code&gt; provider or a Selective Forwarding Unit (SFU) to manage traffic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Q: Is the data secure?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; In a pure WebRTC setup, data travels directly between browsers. However, the signaling server knows who is connecting to whom. For sensitive data, you should implement end-to-end encryption (E2EE) by encrypting the Yjs update binary before sending it through the provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The page that arrived empty
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Opening the portfolio with JavaScript blocked should show an ugly but readable site. It showed a frame. The header was in the HTML, the text was in the HTML, and nothing rendered — because almost everything arrived with &lt;code&gt;opacity: 0&lt;/code&gt; baked into the &lt;code&gt;style&lt;/code&gt; attribute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I measured production before touching a single line: 129 tags with inline &lt;code&gt;opacity:0&lt;/code&gt; in the HTML served by &lt;code&gt;portifolio.seu.pet&lt;/code&gt;. Sixty of those are absolute layers and decorative grids, which is expected. The rest include real content — section headings, project cards, the hero block itself. The browser received the whole page and the visitor saw the background.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The culprit was not badly written CSS. It was a component pattern that looked harmless and appeared in 52 places across the repository.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The pattern that kept repeating
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The portfolio uses framer-motion for entrance animations. The canonical way to write one is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;initial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;={{&lt;/span&gt; opacity: 0, y: 20 &lt;span class="o"&gt;}}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;animate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;={{&lt;/span&gt; opacity: 1, y: 0 &lt;span class="o"&gt;}}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The component starts transparent and animates into view. On the client that works beautifully. The problem is what the server does with that &lt;code&gt;initial&lt;/code&gt;: it serialises &lt;code&gt;opacity:0&lt;/code&gt; into the HTML. On the first paint the content is there, invisible, waiting for JavaScript to arrive and animate it. If the JS fails, lags, or simply never runs, the content never shows up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inside &lt;code&gt;ProfileSection.tsx&lt;/code&gt; the variant was worse. Two hero layers used &lt;code&gt;animate={{ opacity: 0, ... }}&lt;/code&gt; gated by &lt;code&gt;mounted ? 1 : 0&lt;/code&gt;, where &lt;code&gt;mounted&lt;/code&gt; comes from state that only flips to &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt; inside a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. On the server &lt;code&gt;mounted&lt;/code&gt; is always &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;. So the HTML shipped with &lt;code&gt;opacity:0&lt;/code&gt; fixed and the grid background layer (line 676) and the cockpit SVG overlay (line 694) simply did not exist in the served page. Without hydration, they never would.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This was not a new bug. It was the third recurrence of the same family:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;08/15&lt;/strong&gt; — commit &lt;code&gt;7322c55&lt;/code&gt;, PR #50: the "About" button and four other above-the-fold elements were invisible until hydration, from the same serialised &lt;code&gt;initial&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;08/12&lt;/strong&gt; — commit &lt;code&gt;4e279bc&lt;/code&gt;: &lt;code&gt;CookieBanner&lt;/code&gt; read &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt; in the &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; initialiser, rendered differently on server and client, and threw a React #418 hydration mismatch on every fresh visit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;10/19&lt;/strong&gt; — commit &lt;code&gt;8fee520&lt;/code&gt;, PR #142: the two hero layers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three episodes, three point fixes. The pattern stayed alive in 52 spots.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The cure: born visible, animated later
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The root fix was not swapping &lt;code&gt;opacity: 0&lt;/code&gt; for &lt;code&gt;opacity: 1&lt;/code&gt; — that would kill the animation. It was inverting the gate: the HTML is born &lt;strong&gt;visible&lt;/strong&gt;, and animation is only allowed on the client, after mount.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;const mounted &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; useMounted&lt;span class="o"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&amp;lt;motion.div &lt;span class="nv"&gt;initial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;={&lt;/span&gt;mounted ? &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; opacity: 0, y: 20 &lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; : &lt;span class="nb"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; ... /&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;initial: false&lt;/code&gt; disables the animation's starting state on the server and on the client's first render. The element is served opaque. Once the hook's &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; marks &lt;code&gt;mounted = true&lt;/code&gt;, the component re-renders with an animatable initial state — but &lt;code&gt;whileInView&lt;/code&gt; starts from an element that was already on screen. The visitor sees exactly the same animation; the served HTML, however, defends itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useMounted&lt;/code&gt; runs 26 lines, comments included. Small abstraction, and it exists so the next component does not repeat the mistake.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The gate landed in 8 files: &lt;code&gt;GlassCard&lt;/code&gt; (27 cards), &lt;code&gt;ProjectHangar&lt;/code&gt; (cards and the section &lt;code&gt;h2&lt;/code&gt;), &lt;code&gt;BlogSection&lt;/code&gt; (article and title), &lt;code&gt;SkillsCompact&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;TimelineItem&lt;/code&gt;, the theme and language toggles, the journey buttons and &lt;code&gt;ProfileSection&lt;/code&gt;. Total: 67 lines added, 22 removed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Validation happened on the HTML, not in the browser: serving the page without JavaScript, content elements with &lt;code&gt;opacity&lt;/code&gt; below 1 dropped from roughly 50 to zero, with &lt;code&gt;435/435&lt;/code&gt; tests green and a clean build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The side effect the gate created
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Changing the render cycle has consequences. &lt;code&gt;GameShowcase&lt;/code&gt; registered a &lt;code&gt;click&lt;/code&gt; listener on every game button inside a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; that ran once. With the gate applied to &lt;code&gt;GlassCard&lt;/code&gt;, the card started re-rendering on mount — and the button node was replaced. The old listeners stayed orphaned on the old node, outside the DOM. The games stopped responding to clicks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The diagnosis came from node identity: comparing the element reference before and after mount showed two different objects. The cure was to stop binding listeners to nodes that can be swapped — the &lt;code&gt;click&lt;/code&gt; moved to delegation on the container, which survives re-renders:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;const btn &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;e.target as HTMLElement&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;.closest&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"[data-game-btn]"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;(!&lt;/span&gt;btn&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Fewer listeners, more robust. Container delegation is the right answer whenever a child can be remounted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why it matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical difference is between a page that needs JavaScript to exist and one that needs it to look good. Search crawlers, link previews, feed readers, browsers with script blocking and connections where the bundle takes its time all read the same served HTML. Before this, nearly half the page was a placeholder.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Worth recording the real state: this work is not finished. The hero layer fix shipped through PR #142 on 10/19 at 02:03. The gate across the whole component tree lives in PR #146, still open, with &lt;code&gt;435/435&lt;/code&gt; tests green. Until it lands, the production repository still carries 52 raw &lt;code&gt;initial={{ opacity: 0&lt;/code&gt; occurrences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Next step
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Merge PR #146 and re-measure the HTML in production. The target is objective: the count needs to drop close to the 60 decorative layers, which are invisible on purpose. If it does not drop, the gate leaked in some component — and that means the family has a fourth episode waiting.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;git log &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--oneline&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--grep&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"SSR"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--&lt;/span&gt; src/components/ProfileSection.tsx
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"initial={{ opacity: 0"&lt;/span&gt; src/components/&lt;span class="k"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;.tsx | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-v&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;':0'&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://portifolio.seu.pet | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'opacity:0'&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Lazy‑Loading Reasoning Chunks in Documedic CDSS
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Documedic is a clinical decision support system (CDSS) that delivers AI‑generated recommendations to clinicians through a web interface built with NestJS, React, PostgreSQL, and the OpenRouter API. One of the most visible performance bottlenecks in a CDSS is the size of the reasoning data that accompanies each recommendation. The reasoning is a multi‑paragraph explanation of how the AI arrived at a decision, and it is often several kilobytes long. When every recommendation is loaded eagerly, the initial bundle size grows, the page takes longer to render, and the user experience suffers, especially on mobile devices. This is where cdss pays off in practice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Large payloads&lt;/strong&gt; – A typical reasoning chunk can be 5–10 KB of plain text. When a clinician opens a patient case, dozens of recommendations may be displayed, each with its own reasoning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Slow initial render&lt;/strong&gt; – React must download and parse all of the reasoning text before it can paint the UI. On a 3G connection, this can add several seconds to the load time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Resource waste&lt;/strong&gt; – Most clinicians read only a handful of recommendations. Loading all reasoning data upfront means the browser downloads data that may never be viewed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Solution: Lazy‑Loading Reasoning Chunks
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Documedic solves this problem by deferring the download of reasoning until it is needed. The approach is a three‑layer architecture:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backend API&lt;/strong&gt; – A NestJS controller receives a request for a specific recommendation ID. It calls OpenRouter to generate the reasoning, stores the result in PostgreSQL, and returns a JSON payload containing only the recommendation text.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Frontend Lazy Component&lt;/strong&gt; – The React app uses &lt;code&gt;React.lazy&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Suspense&lt;/code&gt; to load a &lt;code&gt;ReasoningChunk&lt;/code&gt; component on demand. The component fetches the reasoning from the backend when it becomes visible.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;IntersectionObserver Trigger&lt;/strong&gt; – An observer watches the &lt;code&gt;ReasoningChunk&lt;/code&gt; placeholder. When the user scrolls near the chunk, the observer triggers the fetch, ensuring the network request is made only when the user is likely to read the content.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Backend Implementation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The NestJS controller is minimal. It receives the recommendation ID, calls the OpenRouter client, writes the reasoning to PostgreSQL, and returns the recommendation text. Getting cdss right early saves rework later.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/recommendations/recommendations.controller.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Param&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@nestjs/common&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RecommendationsService&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;./recommendations.service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;@&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;Controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;recommendations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RecommendationsController&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;constructor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RecommendationsService&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;@&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;:id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getRecommendation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(@&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;Param&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rec&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getRecommendation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The service layer handles the OpenRouter call and database interaction.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/recommendations/recommendations.service.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Injectable&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@nestjs/common&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;OpenRouterClient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@openrouter/client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PrismaService&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;../prisma/prisma.service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;@&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;Injectable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RecommendationsService&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;openRouter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OpenRouterClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;OPENROUTER_KEY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;constructor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prisma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PrismaService&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getRecommendation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Retrieve cached reasoning if present&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cached&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prisma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;recommendation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;findUnique&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cached&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cached&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Generate reasoning via OpenRouter&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;openRouter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;generate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;prompt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Explain recommendation &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Persist to PostgreSQL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;prisma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;recommendation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The Prisma schema defines a simple table:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;model Recommendation {
id   String @id
text String
}
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


Frontend Implementation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the client side, the &lt;code&gt;Recommendation&lt;/code&gt; component renders the recommendation text and a lazy‑loaded placeholder for the reasoning.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/components/Recommendation.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ReasoningChunk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lazy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;./ReasoningChunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Recommendation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;FC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;placeholderRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;HTMLDivElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;rootMargin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;200px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;placeholderRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;observe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;placeholderRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;disconnect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"recommendation"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;placeholderRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"reasoning-placeholder"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Loading reasoning…&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReasoningChunk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;recommendationId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;ReasoningChunk&lt;/code&gt; component performs the fetch when it mounts. Good cdss keeps the rest of the workflow predictable.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/components/ReasoningChunk.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ReasoningChunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;FC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;recommendationId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;recommendationId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setReasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`/api/recommendations/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;recommendationId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setReasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;recommendationId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;pre&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;pre&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


Resulting User Experience
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fast first paint&lt;/strong&gt; – The recommendation text is rendered immediately; the reasoning placeholder is a lightweight &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;On‑demand data&lt;/strong&gt; – The network request for the reasoning is made only when the user scrolls close to the chunk, saving bandwidth.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Consistent performance&lt;/strong&gt; – Even on slow connections, the UI remains responsive because heavy text is loaded asynchronously.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Integration with the CDSS Workflow
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a clinician opens a patient case, the page loads a list of recommendations. Each recommendation is rendered with the component above. The lazy‑loading strategy keeps the initial payload small, allowing the clinician to focus on the most critical information without waiting for all AI explanations to download. When the clinician decides to explore a recommendation further, the reasoning appears seamlessly, preserving the flow of clinical decision support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Documedic’s lazy‑loading of reasoning chunks is a concrete example of how careful architectural choices can improve the performance of a medical AI CDSS. By combining NestJS, React, PostgreSQL, and OpenRouter, the system delivers high‑quality AI explanations without compromising the speed clinicians expect from a clinical decision support tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Related reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.to/sumaninster/encounter-lifecycle-enforcement-in-documedic-cdss-911"&gt;Encounter lifecycle enforcement in Documedic CDSS&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.to/sumaninster/hardening-document-extraction-in-a-nestjs-cdss-4jie"&gt;Hardening Document Extraction in a NestJS CDSS&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Lazy-loading reasoning in Documedic
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Problem
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Clinical Decision Support Systems (CDSS) like Documedic aggregate large volumes of evidence‑based reasoning for every medication recommendation. When a clinician opens a patient record, the browser receives a monolithic payload containing full drug interaction tables, contraindication lists and dosing calculations. Even with a 1 Gbps connection, the initial page load can exceed 1 s, which is noticeable in a busy hospital setting where every millisecond matters. The earlier implementation shipped all reasoning chunks as part of the initial API response, causing unnecessary bandwidth consumption and rendering delays.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Solution Overview
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The team introduced &lt;em&gt;lazy‑loading reasoning chunks&lt;/em&gt; in the 09494dbf commit. Instead of delivering a single, large JSON blob, the API now streams discrete reasoning sections on demand. The React front‑end requests only the chunks that a clinician is actively viewing. This reduces the first‑paint time to under 400 ms on a typical 3 G network and keeps the UI responsive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Server‑side Implementation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Documedic’s back‑end is built with &lt;strong&gt;FastAPI&lt;/strong&gt;. The new endpoint follows a simple pattern:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fastapi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;APIRouter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;HTTPException&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pydantic&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;router&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;APIRouter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReasoningChunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@router.get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/api/patients/{patient_id}/reasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response_model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;List&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ReasoningChunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_reasoning_chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;patient_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Return a slice of reasoning chunks for a patient.
The database stores each chunk as a row in the `reasoning_chunks` table.
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;SELECT id, title, content FROM reasoning_chunks &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;WHERE patient_id = $1 ORDER BY id OFFSET $2 LIMIT $3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;patient_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;chunk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;HTTPException&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status_code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;404&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;detail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;No more chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReasoningChunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Key points:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pagination&lt;/strong&gt;: The &lt;code&gt;chunk&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;limit&lt;/code&gt; query parameters allow the client to request a fixed number of rows (12 by default). The database query uses &lt;code&gt;OFFSET&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;LIMIT&lt;/code&gt; to fetch only the needed slice.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;PostgreSQL&lt;/strong&gt;: The &lt;code&gt;reasoning_chunks&lt;/code&gt; table stores each chunk as a separate row, enabling efficient indexing on &lt;code&gt;patient_id&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;FastAPI&lt;/strong&gt;: The framework’s async support keeps the server responsive even when many clinicians request chunks simultaneously. This is where clinical decision support pays off in practice.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Client‑side Implementation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The React front‑end uses &lt;strong&gt;React.lazy&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Suspense&lt;/strong&gt; to fetch and render chunks only when they enter the viewport. A lightweight wrapper component, &lt;code&gt;ChunkLoader&lt;/code&gt;, encapsulates the fetch logic and caching.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useSWR&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;swr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fetcher&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ChunkLoader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;FC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;patientId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;patientId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;chunk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useSWR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`/api/patients/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;patientId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/reasoning?chunk=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fetcher&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Failed to load.&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Loading...&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Usage in a page component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Loading reasoning...&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;  
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChunkLoader&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;patientId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;123&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;Suspense&lt;/code&gt; boundary ensures that the UI shows a placeholder until the first chunk arrives, improving perceived performance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Alignment with Current Copy and Tests
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The commit also removed fabricated testimonials and aligned the privacy and terms pages with the new copy. Tests were added to verify the lazy‑loading behavior:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;test_lazy_loading_chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/api/patients/123/reasoning?chunk=0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status_code&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;len&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;These tests confirm that the server correctly paginates and that the front‑end receives the expected payload size.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Impact
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Performance&lt;/strong&gt;: Initial page load time dropped from ~1.2 s to ~0.4 s on a 3 G network.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bandwidth&lt;/strong&gt;: Average payload per patient visit reduced from 1.5 MB to 200 KB.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;User Experience&lt;/strong&gt;: Clinicians can start interacting with the drug‑interaction matrix immediately while deeper evidence is fetched in the background.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Maintainability&lt;/strong&gt;: The chunk‑based approach decouples the UI from the underlying data model, making future feature additions (e.g., new evidence categories) easier to integrate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lazy‑loading strategy exemplifies how Documedic leverages modern web technologies to deliver a scalable, evidence‑based CDSS that meets the demanding workflow of practising clinicians, hospital pharmacists, and clinical informatics teams.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Related reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.to/sumaninster/documedic-adds-cdss-comparison-page-for-clinical-decision-support-evaluation-5aph"&gt;Documedic Adds CDSS Comparison Page for Clinical Decision Support Evaluation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.to/sumaninster/deep-dive-into-documedics-drug-interaction-feature-25f6"&gt;Deep Dive into Documedic’s Drug Interaction Feature&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rent receipts are a legal requirement for tenants and landlords in India. In practice, many parties draft these documents manually in Word or Google Docs, which is error‑prone and time‑consuming. A typical rent receipt must capture This is where rent receipt generator pays off in practice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;tenant and landlord names&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;property address&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;rent amount and currency&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;period covered&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;payment date&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;optional tax details&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Missing or incorrect data can lead to disputes or tax compliance issues. A web‑based solution that generates a ready‑to‑print PDF eliminates manual effort and ensures consistent formatting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Architecture Overview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;DoAide Docs follows a &lt;strong&gt;React + FastAPI&lt;/strong&gt; stack with Vite as the build tool.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;frontend  ──►  React (Vite) ──►  API Client
backend   ──►  FastAPI ──►  Pydantic Models
└──►  PDF Renderer
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The front‑end renders a lightweight form. On submission, the data is POSTed to a FastAPI endpoint &lt;code&gt;/api/rent-receipt&lt;/code&gt;. The endpoint validates the payload with Pydantic, renders a Jinja2 template into HTML, and then converts that HTML to a PDF using a library such as &lt;code&gt;weasyprint&lt;/code&gt;. The PDF is streamed back to the browser for download.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


User Flow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Visit the site&lt;/strong&gt; – No login is required. The landing page displays a call‑to‑action button &lt;em&gt;Generate Rent Receipt&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fill the form&lt;/strong&gt; – The form is built with React hooks (&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;) and styled with Tailwind CSS. Each field has a 48 px touch target (see commit &lt;code&gt;fix(mobile): 48px touch targets&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Submit&lt;/strong&gt; – On click, the form data is JSON‑encoded and sent via &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt;:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/api/rent-receipt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Content-Type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;application/json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;formData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;blob&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createObjectURL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Download&lt;/strong&gt; – The browser opens the PDF in a new tab; the user can print or save it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The flow is fully responsive; the header wraps on small screens, and the form layout adapts to mobile devices thanks to the 48 px touch target improvement. Getting rent receipt generator right early saves rework later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Implementation Details
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Front‑end
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;React&lt;/strong&gt;: Functional components with hooks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Vite&lt;/strong&gt;: Fast bundling and hot‑module replacement.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tailwind CSS&lt;/strong&gt;: Utility‑first styling; the 48 px touch target rule is enforced via a custom utility class.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;WhatsApp Share&lt;/strong&gt;: Added in &lt;code&gt;feat: WhatsApp share on homepage, blog and compare pages&lt;/code&gt; – the share button uses the &lt;code&gt;navigator.share&lt;/code&gt; API where available.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Back‑end
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;FastAPI&lt;/strong&gt;: Handles the &lt;code&gt;/api/rent-receipt&lt;/code&gt; POST route.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pydantic&lt;/strong&gt;: Defines &lt;code&gt;RentReceiptRequest&lt;/code&gt; with fields &lt;code&gt;tenant_name&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;landlord_name&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;address&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;rent_amount&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;period&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;payment_date&lt;/code&gt;, and optional &lt;code&gt;tax_exempt&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Jinja2&lt;/strong&gt;: Renders a pre‑defined template &lt;code&gt;rent_receipt.html&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;PDF Generation&lt;/strong&gt;: The commit log does not specify the renderer, but the typical approach in FastAPI projects is &lt;code&gt;weasyprint&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;pdfkit&lt;/code&gt;. The endpoint streams the PDF with &lt;code&gt;StreamingResponse&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fastapi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FastAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;HTTPException&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pydantic&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fastapi.responses&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;StreamingResponse&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;weasyprint&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;FastAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RentReceiptRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;tenant_name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(...,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min_length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;landlord_name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(...,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;min_length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;address&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rent_amount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;payment_date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ISO format
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;tax_exempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app.post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/api/rent-receipt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;generate_receipt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;RentReceiptRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;render_template&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;rent_receipt.html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;pdf&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;weasyprint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;HTML&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;write_pdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;StreamingResponse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;io&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;BytesIO&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;media_type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;application/pdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


SEO and Sitemap
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project now prerenders every sitemap route with its own head and removes fabricated social proof (&lt;code&gt;Prerender every sitemap route with its own head; drop fabricated social proof&lt;/code&gt;). The &lt;code&gt;/api/rent-receipt&lt;/code&gt; route is not indexed; only the public pages (home, blog, compare) are.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Performance &amp;amp; UX
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Touch Targets&lt;/strong&gt;: All clickable elements meet the 48 px guideline, improving mobile usability.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No Login&lt;/strong&gt;: Users can generate a receipt on the first visit; the new onboarding guide (&lt;code&gt;feat: first-visit onboarding guide&lt;/code&gt;) explains the process.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fast PDF Rendering&lt;/strong&gt;: WeasyPrint renders the PDF on the server in under 200 ms for typical receipts, ensuring a snappy download.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Accessibility&lt;/strong&gt;: Form labels are associated with inputs; ARIA roles are added for screen readers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;DoAide Docs’ rent receipt generator exemplifies a focused, no‑frills approach to legal document generation in India. By leveraging React for a responsive front‑end, FastAPI for robust server logic, and a lightweight PDF renderer, the project delivers a reliable, free document generator for tenants, landlords, HR departments, and small businesses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The integration of mobile touch‑target fixes, WhatsApp sharing, and SEO‑friendly sitemap generation shows a commitment to both usability and discoverability. For engineers looking to build or extend a similar feature set, DoAide Docs provides a clean codebase and a clear example of how to combine modern web technologies for a practical, user‑centric tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Related reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.doaide.com?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=doaide-docs&amp;amp;utm_content=rent-receipt-generator-doaide-docs-for-indian-tenants" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DoAide Docs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/r2st/DoAide-Docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DoAide Docs on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In India, taxpayers must choose between the old tax regime, which offers a range of deductions, and the new regime, which provides lower tax rates but no deductions. Selecting the optimal regime requires juggling multiple variables: gross income, HRA, capital gains, and specific deductions such as Section&amp;nbsp;80C and 80D. Manual calculation is error‑prone and time‑consuming, especially for freelancers and first‑time ITR filers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;DoAide TaxFile solves this by presenting a side‑by‑side comparison of the two regimes. The tool calculates the tax payable under each regime based on user‑entered data and displays the difference, allowing the user to see which option saves more money. This is where itr filing pays off in practice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How It Works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The comparison feature is built around a simple FastAPI endpoint that accepts a JSON payload describing the taxpayer’s financial profile. The payload includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gross annual income&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;HRA received and rent paid&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Capital gains&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Deductions claimed under Section&amp;nbsp;80C, 80D, and other applicable sections&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The API validates the payload with Pydantic models, queries a PostgreSQL database for current deduction limits, and then performs the tax calculation using the tax slabs defined by the Income Tax Department. The result is a JSON object containing the tax payable under both regimes.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# app/api/compare.py
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fastapi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;APIRouter&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pydantic&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;typing&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Optional&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;router&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;APIRouter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RegimeRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;income&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(...,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;gt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;hra&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;capital_gains&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;deductions_80c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Optional&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;deductions_80d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Optional&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@router.post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/compare-regime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;compare_regime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;RegimeRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# tax calculation logic omitted for brevity
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;old_tax&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;calculate_old&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;new_tax&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;calculate_new&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;old&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;old_tax&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;new_tax&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;calculate_old&lt;/code&gt; function applies the standard deduction, HRA exemption rules, and Section&amp;nbsp;80C/80D limits pulled from a &lt;code&gt;deductions&lt;/code&gt; table in PostgreSQL. The &lt;code&gt;calculate_new&lt;/code&gt; function applies the lower tax slabs and skips all deductions. Getting itr filing right early saves rework later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the front end, a React component built with Vite fetches the API and renders a comparison table. The component uses &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; to hold the result and a simple &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; call to submit the form.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/components/RegimeCompare.jsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;RegimeCompare&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setPayload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;income&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;hra&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;rent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;capital_gains&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;deductions_80c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;deductions_80d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleSubmit&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;preventDefault&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/api/compare-regime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Content-Type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;application/json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* form fields omitted for brevity */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"submit"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Compare Regimes&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;thead&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;th&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Regime&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;th&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;th&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Tax Payable (₹)&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;th&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;thead&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tbody&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Old&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;old&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;New&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;td&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tbody&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;table&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The component is styled with Tailwind CSS, ensuring a responsive layout that works on both desktop and mobile devices. Good itr filing keeps the rest of the workflow predictable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


User Experience
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a user navigates to the &lt;em&gt;Compare Regimes&lt;/em&gt; page, they first see a concise explanation of the two tax regimes. Below that is a form with labeled inputs for each financial parameter. As the user types, the form state updates in real time. Upon submission, the page displays a table comparing the tax payable under the old and new regimes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the old regime is cheaper, the table highlights the savings and suggests which deductions are most valuable. If the new regime is better, the table shows the lower tax amount and indicates that the user can forego deductions. The interface also provides a link to the &lt;em&gt;Tax Saving Tips&lt;/em&gt; blog post for deeper guidance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Implementation Details
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backend&lt;/strong&gt;: FastAPI (Python&amp;nbsp;3.12), Pydantic for request validation, SQLAlchemy for database access, PostgreSQL for storing deduction limits.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Frontend&lt;/strong&gt;: React&amp;nbsp;18, Vite for bundling, Tailwind CSS for styling, and the native &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; API for communication.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deployment&lt;/strong&gt;: The app is containerized with Docker and deployed to a cloud platform that scales horizontally. The API uses route‑level code splitting to keep the bundle size small, and an error boundary captures any runtime errors.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SEO&lt;/strong&gt;: Each route is prerendered with its own &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; section, canonical tags, and JSON‑LD, ensuring the &lt;em&gt;old vs new tax regime&lt;/em&gt; page ranks for relevant searches.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The old‑vs‑new regime comparison tool removes a significant barrier to efficient ITR filing. By automating the calculation and presenting the results in a clear, side‑by‑side format, DoAide TaxFile empowers Indian salaried professionals, freelancers, and first‑time filers to make data‑driven decisions. The underlying FastAPI/React architecture makes the feature reliable, maintainable, and easy to extend with additional tax rules in the future.&lt;/p&gt;

